<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/roof-shingles.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/roof-shingles-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/roof-shingles.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="720" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/hawaii-claim-guides/">Claim guides</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">General Excise Tax and Freight in Hawaii Repair Estimates</span></nav><p class="tag">Claim guide · 7 min read</p><h1>General Excise Tax and Freight in Hawaii Repair Estimates</h1><p class="lede">Hawaii’s general excise tax, ocean freight and island labor can add real money to a repair. Here is how estimates should account for them.</p></div></section><div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><p class="byline">By the Hawaii Insurance Appraiser team · <time datetime="2026-11-10">November 10, 2026</time> · 7 min read</p>
<h2>Why island costs belong in the estimate</h2>
<p>A repair estimate is supposed to reflect what it actually costs to fix the damage where the property sits. In Hawaii, that means accounting for costs a mainland-style estimate may not capture well: the general excise tax, ocean freight to the islands, inter-island barge charges and the realities of local labor. When those costs are missing or handled inconsistently, the gap between the insurer’s number and your contractor’s bid can be significant.</p>

<h2>How the general excise tax works</h2>
<p>Hawaii does not have a traditional sales tax. Instead, it imposes a general excise tax (GET) on the gross income of businesses, including contractors. Although the tax is legally on the business, contractors commonly pass it on to customers as a line on the invoice.</p>
<p>The rate depends on the county. The state rate is 4%, and most counties add a surcharge, so the combined rate is roughly 4% to about 4.7% depending on where the work is done. Check the current rate for your county, since surcharges can change.</p>
<p>Because GET is calculated on gross income, the way a contractor passes it on can result in an effective rate slightly higher than the nominal rate. Estimates do not need to be perfect on this point, but they should be consistent.</p>

<h2>How GET should appear in an estimate</h2>
<p>Problems arise when GET is treated differently on two estimates being compared. Common issues include:</p>
<ul>
<li>The insurer’s estimate applies tax only to materials, as a mainland sales tax would, while the contractor’s bid applies GET to the entire invoice, including labor.</li>
<li>One estimate omits GET entirely.</li>
<li>Tax is applied before overhead and profit on one estimate and after on the other.</li>
<li>The county rate used does not match the island where the work will be done.</li>
</ul>
<p>Here is an illustrative example only: on a $50,000 repair, the difference between applying a roughly 4.5% GET to the full invoice versus only to $20,000 of materials is more than $1,300. On larger claims, the difference grows accordingly. The point is not the specific number but the need to compare like with like.</p>

<h2>Freight and inter-island barge</h2>
<p>Almost all building materials in Hawaii arrive by ocean freight. Roofing panels, drywall, cabinets, windows and specialty items are shipped from the mainland or overseas, then moved to the job site. For properties on Kauai, Maui, Molokai, Lanai or the Big Island, many materials must also travel by inter-island barge from Oahu.</p>
<p>Estimating software pricing may or may not fully capture these costs for a given island and material. Things to look for in an estimate include:</p>
<ol>
<li>Whether unit prices reflect the island where the work will be done, not just a statewide or regional average.</li>
<li>Whether freight, barge and delivery charges are included, either built into unit prices or as separate line items.</li>
<li>Whether special-order items, such as matching metal roof panels or custom windows, include shipping and longer lead times.</li>
<li>Whether disposal costs reflect local landfill and transfer station realities, which differ by island.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Island labor availability</h2>
<p>Labor is another island-specific cost. Neighbor islands have smaller pools of licensed contractors and specialty trades. After a regional event – a hurricane, a Kona low, a wildfire – demand spikes and qualified crews may be booked for months. Some contractors fly in workers from other islands, adding travel and lodging costs.</p>
<p>An estimate that assumes mainland labor rates, or Honolulu rates on a remote Big Island property, may understate the real cost. Good documentation includes multiple local bids where possible and notes on lead times and availability.</p>

<div class="note"><b class="h">Consistency is the goal</b>Appraisal panels usually do better comparing estimates that treat GET, freight and labor the same way. When you present your contractor’s bid, ask the contractor to show how tax and freight are handled so your appraiser can reconcile it with the insurer’s estimate.</div>

<h2>Overhead and profit</h2>
<p>General contractor overhead and profit (O&amp;P) is another frequent dispute. Larger or multi-trade repairs commonly involve a general contractor who coordinates work and charges O&amp;P. In Hawaii, where coordinating trades, freight and permits can take substantial effort, O&amp;P is often a legitimate cost. Whether it is owed under a given policy and claim depends on the policy and the circumstances.</p>

<h2>Using these points in a dispute</h2>
<p>If you believe the insurer’s estimate left out GET, freight or island labor costs, start by asking the adjuster to explain how those costs were handled and to send the pricing assumptions in writing. Many differences can be resolved through a supplement. If they cannot, an appraisal panel can value the loss with these costs considered.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/appraisal-specialties/carrier-estimate-review/">carrier estimate review</a> service looks specifically at pricing, tax and freight issues like these. You can also use the <a href="/island-claim-tools/claim-gap-fee-estimator/">claim gap fee estimator</a> to see whether the difference is large enough to justify appraisal. For related reading, see our guide on <a href="/hawaii-claim-guides/salt-air-corrosion-vs-storm-damage/">salt air corrosion vs storm damage</a>.</p>
<h2>Quick answers</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Should GET be applied to labor in a repair estimate?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Contractors commonly pass GET on to customers on the whole invoice, since the tax applies to their gross income. The key is that estimates being compared treat it consistently.</p></div></details><details><summary>What GET rate should an estimate use?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>The rate for the county where the work is done, roughly 4% to about 4.7%. Check the current county rate, since surcharges can change.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are freight and barge costs part of the amount of loss?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Freight and delivery are part of what it costs to repair on an island, so a complete estimate should account for them, either in unit prices or as separate line items.</p></div></details></div><h2>Keep reading</h2><div class="related"><a href="/hawaii-claim-guides/hurricane-deductibles-in-hawaii/"><small>Guide</small><span>Hurricane Deductibles in Hawaii: How They Work and What to Check</span><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/hawaii-claim-guides/condo-master-policy-vs-ho6/"><small>Guide</small><span>Condo